Yes. What you’re describing is stronger than “copy the successful parts.” It is extract the experiential topology, remove the source-domain surface, then reinstantiate the topology in a new world.

Pokémon is a particularly clean specimen because the experience is distributed across many layers at once: discovery, collection, taxonomy, rarity, bonding, evolution, completion pressure, trading, comparison, identity, nostalgia, surprise, recognizable silhouettes, bright categorical color, constrained mystery, recurring rituals, social proof, and an endlessly extensible creature-space. The brand is not any one of those things; the felt system comes from their composition.

And your timing point is real: 2026 is Pokémon’s official 30-year celebration, with anniversary products and events continuing through the year, including the current 30th Celebration TCG release. 

The interesting move is to factor the experience into primitives that are more general than Pokémon itself. Something like:

unknown world → bounded discoverable entities → encounter → recognition → acquisition → naming/classification → ownership/bond → differentiation/rarity → growth/transformation → set completion → social comparison/exchange → new unknowns

Then overlay the affective layer:

wonder + anticipation + ownership + competence + attachment + scarcity + surprise + completion tension + nostalgia + belonging

Then the sensory/interface layer:

high discriminability + strong silhouettes + categorical variation + recurring reveal rituals + compact symbolic metadata + collection surfaces + visible progress + periodic novelty

Now you have something much closer to an experiential algorithm than a franchise imitation.

That algorithm could be re-instantiated in organisms, rocks, historical artifacts, foods, astronomical objects, skills, local places, AI agents, scientific phenomena, even something like your Field Codex. Imagine encountering an unfamiliar mushroom in the woods and the system treats the event with the same psychological geometry as discovering an unfamiliar creature: identify it, classify it, learn its “abilities,” habitat, rarity, relationships, variants, evolutionary relatives, and add it to an accumulating living atlas. Nothing needs to look remotely like Pokémon, yet the discovery/collection/bonding circuitry can rhyme with it.

That is very close to several Pantheon pieces clicking together:

Program Archetype Transduction extracts the computational anatomy.
Affective Simulation extracts the desired felt-state.
Substrate Escape lets the structure leave Pokémon entirely.
Semantic Primes gives us the irreducible pieces.
Pattern Sans Echoes helps distinguish the universal attractors from arbitrary franchise-specific expression.

And there is an important distinction that makes the method much more powerful anyway: don’t try to make something “secretly Pokémon.” That leaves you chained to Pokémon and risks reproducing distinctive protected expression. Strip farther. Things like “collect diverse entities,” “discover rare variants,” “form attachments,” “complete sets,” and “exchange discoveries” are broad mechanics; specific characters, terminology, distinctive art elements, catchphrases, logos, and close trade-dress imitation are a different matter.

The target is therefore not:

Pokémon → disguise Pokémon

but:

Pokémon → infer why humans respond → recover general generators → recombine generators → produce a genuinely new phenotype

And here is the fun part: once you do this across many beloved systems rather than one, you can factor them against each other.

Take Pokémon, LEGO, Disney parks, baseball cards, Tamagotchi, Zelda, Costco treasure-hunting, geocaching, fantasy football, museums, Reddit, slot-machine reveal mechanics, national parks passports, etc. Strip each one. Then look for recurrent primitives.

What survives across unrelated systems is increasingly unlikely to be merely “Pokémon-ness.” Those survivors are candidates for human engagement invariants.

Then you don’t have a Pokémon algorithm.

You have something closer to a periodic table of experiential design—and Pokémon is merely one exceptionally successful molecule made from those elements.